data.content:
This is the **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

## What happened

In C# 14, new built-in conversions from arrays to `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` mean that `array.Contains(num)` now resolves to `MemoryExtensions.Contains` (a span-based method) instead of `Enumerable.Contains`. Since `MemoryExtensions.Contains` **cannot be represented in a LINQ expression tree**, it throws at runtime when the expression is compiled with `preferInterpretation: true`.

## How to fix

Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 3: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

All three options ensure the compiler selects the expression-tree-compatible `Enumerable.Contains` overload.
